---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Stores Designer — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Design-system tooling at Amazon: helping many teams build one consistent shopping surface without each of them coding it by hand."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03 — "each case study gets the same
       pinned column, with its own ledger already written on the page"). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the site's
       one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study.
       On a TYPE A page the masthead ledger moves into the panel: it is metadata read
       BEFORE the story, which is exactly what a column that never leaves the screen is
       for. The dek, the spec block, and every section stay in the reading column. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/stores-designer/" title="Stores Designer">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">Frontend engineer — the tooling, and the design system underneath it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Where</div>
        <!-- No `<b>`. Annotation: "dont think this needs to be stronger than 'at amazon'" — the
             employer is the fact worth reading here and the date range was the emphasised half,
             which inverted the row. Neither is emphasised now; the label above them says what the
             pair is. -->
        <div class="ftx">At Amazon · 2023–present</div>
      </div>
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap (512px)
           is narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px anyway, so c1–8 would have set a column
           the text never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column,
           and it lines up with the back link at the top of the panel. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          Design-system tooling at Amazon. A lot of teams build one shopping surface, and my
          job is to make sure they don’t each build it their own way.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A with no clip: this is current production work at Amazon, so there is nothing
           public to show. `.specblock` takes beat 2 instead (chooser round 2, Q2/02) — a mono
           key/value listing of what the thing is made of, which argues the engineering in the
           slot a recording would have held without putting anything internal on a public host.
           It carries no screenshot, no component name, no metric, and no architecture: every
           line here is already stated elsewhere on this page or in the dek.
           There is no `i build` row, though it was the obvious one to write: the panel's
           ledger opens with "Role — the tooling, and the design system underneath it," and
           printing the same sentence twice on one screen makes a reader stop to check whether
           the two differ. The ledger is the row that has to be there (Type A), so this block
           is the one that gives it up.
           THE "No clip: production work at Amazon" CAPTION IS DELETED. It was a `figcaption` on a
           block with no figure — a label for an absent thing, which made the page open by naming
           what it doesn't have. The fact it stated is already the block's own last row
           ("visibility — internal"), so it was also the same fact twice.
           A CLIP IS COMING and the plan is decided: a capture of the real UI driven by an invented
           dataset — fake brand names, placeholder copy, no component names, no metrics, no real
           campaign — so the tool can be shown working with nothing disclosable on screen. When
           `public/media/sd-fakeset.mp4` exists, it goes in a `figure.demo.hang` immediately above
           this block, and `.mscatter`'s 1000/626 crop applies so it matches every other clip on
           the site. Until then the page says nothing about it, which is better than apologising. -->
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <div class="specblock">
          <div class="srow"><span class="sk">surface</span>
            <span class="sv">one shopping surface, many teams</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span class="sk">constraint</span>
            <span class="sv">a wrong component ships to a lot of pages at once</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span class="sk">approach</span>
            <span class="sv"><i>the consistent way to ship a page is also the fastest way</i></span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span class="sk">visibility</span>
            <span class="sv">internal — the public version of this problem is Luthier</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The problem</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Many teams, one shopping surface. Left alone, each team builds its own version
              of the same component and the surface drifts apart — slightly different
              spacing, slightly different behaviour, a hundred times over.
            </p>
            <p>
              Telling people to be consistent doesn’t work; nobody has time to check.
              Building the shared thing so it’s the quickest route to a shipped page does.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A, the alternatives beat (round-3 sheet, Q05). This page has no numbers to
           publish, so the alternatives ARE its argument: two approaches to the same problem,
           one of which I'd already tried. Nothing internal — both options are stated at the
           level of the dek. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>What else was on the table</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The usual answer is governance: a documented standard, a review step, and
              someone to enforce it. It fails for a reason that isn’t about discipline — the
              reviewer is a bottleneck on every team at once, and the standard is a document
              nobody reads at the moment they’re shipping.
            </p>
            <p>
              The other answer is the one I’d already built at <a href="/work/luthier/">Luthier</a>:
              make the shared thing the fastest route to a finished page, so the consistent
              version is the one a team reaches for. That’s the approach here, on a bigger
              surface — and the public version of the problem is that earlier page, because
              this one stays internal.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A close: named, last. "Where it stands" rather than "Outcome" — this work is
           still running, and an "Outcome" head on unfinished work overclaims. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Where it stands</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Front-end for the tooling, and the design system it builds against. Most of what
              I’ve learned about working with agents came out of this: the loops on <a href="/method/">the method page</a> are the ones I run here, on a system
              where a wrong component ships to a lot of pages at once.
            </p>
            <p class="fine">
              Production work at Amazon, so this page stays high level. No internal detail.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
